# wfg-log 1
# fixture triggers — three cues fired by three things that are not a person,
# hand-written and hand-checked.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. §3.7 lets a cue be fired by a message on Go.dot's
# own OSC port, by a MIDI event on an input it was told to open, or by a time of
# day. None of those exists on a machine replaying a log: there is no socket, no
# MIDI interface, and it is a different hour on a different day.
#
# Which is exactly why a trigger firing is a COMMAND. The matching happens
# outside the model — on a socket thread, on a driver callback, in the serve
# loop — and what reaches the engine is `trigger.fire <trigger>`, a record like
# any other. So this file reproduces three inputs that are not present, on a
# machine that has none of them.
#
# THE ORIGIN SAYS WHERE IT CAME FROM AND NOTHING ELSE. Nothing anywhere enforces
# an origin, by design (RunCommands.h: a command only the inside of the process
# could send would be one a replay could not send), so the trigger's identity
# travels as the ARGUMENT — where it can be checked — and `udp:`, `midi:` and
# `clock` are there for the person reading the log at two in the morning.
#
# AND THE STANDBY DOES NOT MOVE. Not once, anywhere below. §3.5 and §3.7 both
# say it, and it is the reason a background list can be driven by something
# other than a person without the person losing their place. The pointer is on
# `TRG00002` when this log begins and it is on `TRG00002` when it ends; nothing
# in the file says so, which is the point — no record moved it because no
# command that moves it was sent.
#
# A LIGHTING DESK sends `/desk/chime`. The trigger's identifier is the argument;
# the run identifier is the one the fire drew, recorded so a replay is handed it
# rather than drawing one of its own.
A 10 0 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 trigger.fire s:"TRG00012" s:"RN000001"
#
# A memo ends on the tick after it fires, as it does whoever fired it.
A 11 1 engine run.ended s:"RN000001"
#
# THE SAME TRIGGER AGAIN, forty ticks later, because a desk that sends a message
# once will send it again — and a second run is what §6.6 decision 3 asks for on
# a memo: two instances, both live, rather than one ignored.
A 50 2 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 trigger.fire s:"TRG00012" s:"RN000002"
A 51 3 engine run.ended s:"RN000002"
#
# 19:30:00 ARRIVES. The engine did not notice this — nothing inside it reads a
# wall clock, which is what makes a replay reproducible (Engine.h). The serve
# loop read the clock, saw a second crossed, and submitted. On replay there is
# no clock in the room and the record is all there is.
A 90 4 clock trigger.fire s:"TRG00014" s:"RN000003"
A 91 5 engine run.ended s:"RN000003"
#
# AND A MIDI EVENT, from an input this machine does not have. The record is
# identical in shape to the two above it, which is the whole argument for
# putting the matching outside the model: three quite different pieces of
# hardware, one command, one thing to replay.
#
# The trigger it names is the OSC one, deliberately — `trigger.fire` fires the
# cue a trigger sits on and asks nothing about how the news arrived, so a MIDI
# origin firing an OSC trigger is legal and is what a `wfg replay` of a
# hand-edited log would do. What it is not is a second road into the engine: the
# matcher that produced this on the night matched a MIDI trigger.
A 120 6 midi:BCF2000 trigger.fire s:"TRG00012" s:"RN000004"
A 121 7 engine run.ended s:"RN000004"
#
# AND ONE THAT IS REFUSED. A trigger identifier that names nothing is not a
# thing to guess at: a replay of a log whose show has since had a trigger
# deleted must say so rather than firing whatever is nearest.
R 150 8 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 unknown-id trigger.fire s:"NOSUCHID"
#
# And a cue's identifier is refused too, which is the same rule seen from the
# other side: `trigger.fire` takes the TRIGGER, and firing a cue by name is
# `cue.fire`. Two commands with two meanings rather than one that guesses.
R 160 9 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 unknown-id trigger.fire s:"TRG00011"
